>[conklin@frith.uucp (Terry Conklin) writes:]
...
>[] Drowing in pools is no better. To arbitarily lose a character ("Wham.
>[] You die") because you moved your hands (noting that the nethack keys are
>[] NOT 'home row') or such. Everything else is a thinking situation, why
>[] this demigod-is-totally-instantly-irrevocably-overwelmed-by-wading-pool
>[] bit? It serves no purpose.
>
>except to keep the game threatening ...
>
>A pool of water is a _real_ threat to your game and you _must_ be prepared
>for it when it enters into the game. Carelessness _will_ get you killed,
>that's a feature ...

There's a difference between careless strategy and klutzy fingers.
Confirmations are meant to guard against the latter, in my opinion.
Dave, do you think that all the confirmations are inappropriate?
If not, why should the game help you guard against, say, attacking
friendly monsters, but not against walking into pools?
It seems to me that pools are more obvious dangers
and so it is at least as appropriate to do confirmation on them.

The overeating bit I can live with.  You can reliably avoid choking by
never eating when satiated.  The problem is, many people think that you
can avoid choking by always stopping when it says "Stop eating? [yn]"
Players will hopefully only die this way once, and then adjust their
expectations.

